diff --git a/REJECTED.md b/REJECTED.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..eb207736fa46dc0a89623d75ad3eca7498485d76
--- /dev/null
+++ b/REJECTED.md
@@ -0,0 +1,139 @@
+# Rejected at Step 0: the explicit labeling has no Track B compression gap
+
+Paper: An Cao, Aleyah Dawkins, Julian Hutchins, and Orlando Luce,
+[“Radio gracefulness of Moore graphs and beyond”](https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00228),
+arXiv:2510.00228v1.
+
+## Decision
+
+No generator is shipped. The paper-native labeling problems can be generatable and
+exactly verifiable, but the scalable cases with an explicit certificate fail **H on
+Track A**, and they also fail **H on Track B** after comparing the mechanical and
+compact costs. The alternatives either do not produce a certificate by construction,
+have only finitely many hand-listed certificates, or obtain apparent hardness by
+inserting an unrelated Hamiltonian-path distribution for which the paper proves no
+distributional hardness.
+
+This is not a witness-rule rejection. A radio labeling is an excellent concrete
+witness: check that its labels have the required range and check
+
+\[
+  |f(u)-f(v)|+d(u,v)\geq \operatorname{diam}(G)+1
+\]
+
+for every vertex pair. The problem is how cheaply the paper itself produces that
+witness.
+
+## What the paper actually says
+
+Definition 2.2 defines a radio labeling and calls it graceful when its span is the
+number of vertices. Theorem 2.4 states that, for a diameter-2 graph, radio gracefulness
+is equivalent to the complement (the antipodal graph) having a Hamiltonian path.
+Theorem 3.2 gives the analogous necessary-and-sufficient condition for bipartite
+diameter-3 graphs. Thus, ordering the vertices along the relevant Hamiltonian path and
+assigning consecutive labels is the certificate.
+
+The main scalable native examples are not opaque search instances:
+
+- Theorem 3.7 and Remark 3.8 handle incidence graphs of projective planes by a
+  Hamiltonian path in the non-incidence graph.
+- Theorems 3.9 and 3.14 make every bipartite graph of even diameter immediately
+  non-radio-graceful; Theorems 3.12 and 3.17 then obtain optimum spans for generalized
+  quadrangle and generalized hexagon incidence graphs from powers of Hamiltonian
+  cycles.
+- Theorem 3.21 and Theorem 3.32 prove that the Erdős–Rényi polarity graph and its
+  complement are radio graceful.
+- Most decisively, Constructions 3.33 and 3.34 write out the graceful labeling of the
+  Singer-graph representation of those polarity graphs as an alternating modular
+  recurrence. Construction 3.34 starts with a permitted pair `d0,d1` and then computes
+  each next vertex with one subtraction modulo `q^2+q+1`.
+
+The paper's easy regimes are equally explicit. Theorems 2.5 and 2.6 give dense-graph
+degree conditions for a Hamiltonian path; Proposition 3.4 applies the bipartite
+condition; Porism 3.22 makes every diameter-2 graph with maximum degree at most half
+the order radio graceful; and Corollary 3.25 applies that test to every MMS graph.
+Theorem 3.6 even completely classifies the `(n-1)`- and `(n-2)`-regular bipartite
+diameter-3 boundary cases through their antipodal graphs.
+
+## Step-0 mechanical cost versus compact route
+
+The most favorable proposed Track B family is Construction 3.34: provide a Singer
+difference set and ask for a graceful radio labeling of the complement of its Singer
+graph. Let `N=q^2+q+1`. The reference algorithm stated in the paper is:
+
+1. find a permitted pair `d0,d1` in the supplied difference set,
+2. compute the forced initial residue (one modular multiplication), and
+3. emit the remaining `N-1` residues by the displayed alternating modular recurrence.
+
+Its complexity is **O(N) time and O(N) output**, not Hamiltonian-path search. At the
+largest prime-power parameter whose full labeling fits the 256-atom answer cap,
+`q=13` and `N=183`. The difference-set property guarantees that residue `1` occurs as
+an ordered difference, so a set plus at most `q+1=14` modular-successor membership
+probes finds a pair with gcd-one difference. The recurrence itself is exactly one
+initialization plus **182 modular updates**, for at most 197 high-level operations
+after the 14-element set has been read. A CPython 3 benchmark over 400,000 complete
+recurrence evaluations measured a median of **0.000028924 seconds per labeling**
+(pair selection excluded). The next prime power is `q=16`, for which `N=273`, already
+beyond the atomic-element cap.
+
+| Route at `q=13`, `N=183` | Mechanical cost | Compact route | Track B result |
+|---|---:|---:|---|
+| Construction 3.34 for `complement(ER_q)` | At most 14 pair probes + 1 initialization + 182 recurrence updates; O(N); recurrence kernel 0.000028924 s measured | **The identical pair observation and recurrence**, including the same 183 output values | Fail: cost ratio 1; nothing is compressed |
+| Construction 3.33 for `ER_q` | The same O(N) alternating recurrence after choosing its displayed constants; its complement-membership choices are also linear scans | **The identical recurrence and membership observations** | Fail: no shorter solver-visible route |
+| Verify either full labeling | At most all vertex-pair distance/inequality checks, polynomial in N | No bearing on witness search; the producer above is already linear | V passes, H does not |
+
+This remains a rejection rather than `cap_bound`. Increasing `q` only makes the same
+linear recurrence and the required transcript longer. It does not open a fixed-length
+hardness axis, and the compact route never becomes shorter than the mechanical one.
+The obstruction is the absence of a Track B compression gap, not merely the current
+answer cap.
+
+A succinct answer does not rescue the family. If the answer is only the recurrence
+parameters from Construction 3.34, a structure-aware candidate must already choose
+`d0,d1` from the supplied difference set with `gcd(d0-d1,N)=1`, because those
+conditions are stated in the problem and are trivial to enforce. **Every such
+candidate is a valid witness**, so the G4 hit rate is 1, not below `1e-6`.
+Construction 3.33 has the same defect once its displayed non-membership and gcd
+conditions are built into the candidate sampler. Sampling arbitrary residues instead
+would be precisely the naive-space error forbidden by G4.
+
+## Candidate-family audit
+
+| Candidate task | G | H | V / other gate | Outcome |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|---|
+| Full graceful labeling of `ER_q` or its complement | Pass by Constructions 3.33–3.34 | **Fail A:** explicit linear algorithm. **Fail B:** mechanical and compact routes are the same recurrence | Exact pairwise verification; 183 atoms at largest admissible `q` | Reject |
+| Succinct recurrence parameters | Pass | The parameters directly instantiate the displayed construction | **Fail G4:** every structure-aware candidate is valid | Reject |
+| Projective-plane incidence graph labeling | Theorem 3.7 proves existence, but its proof invokes the Hamiltonian-path theorem and does not output a path; implementing search would obtain the certificate by solving the generated instance | No paper-backed hard distribution | Verification is exact | Reject |
+| Optimal labeling for generalized quadrangle/hexagon cages | For general `q`, Theorems 3.12 and 3.17 invoke existence of powers of Hamiltonian cycles rather than an explicit scalable cycle; `q=2,3` are hand-listed finite cases | No scalable planted certificate and no distributional hardness theorem | Full labelings exceed the answer cap quickly | Reject |
+| Certified non-gracefulness for even-diameter bipartite graphs | A bipartition/antipodal disconnection certificate is easy to build | **Fail A and B:** BFS, bipartition, and distance checks produce it in polynomial time, with no distinct shortcut | Small scalar claims are guessable; larger certificates do not repair H | Reject |
+| Arbitrary diameter-3 bipartite graph with a planted antipodal Hamiltonian path | Pass by inverse generation using Theorem 3.2 | Unsupported: the paper proves an equivalence, not hardness for any planted distribution; random planted Hamiltonian graphs may have many easy paths | Exact verification is possible | Reject as a new external benchmark, not this paper's proved regime |
+| Random relabelings of a fixed `ER_q` | Pass by carrying the labeling through the relabeling | Still solved by undoing/recognizing the same construction, or else has no compact route | **Fail G8 diversity:** relabelings have the same canonical key | Reject |
+
+## Why generic Hamiltonian-path hardness is not enough
+
+Theorems 2.4 and 3.2 make Hamiltonian paths central, but worst-case Hamiltonian-path
+hardness does not establish hardness for the paper's polarity graphs, dense
+antipodal graphs, or a builder-chosen planted distribution. The paper supplies no
+average-case or parameterized-hardness theorem. Replacing its explicit graph families
+with an external collection of hard Hamiltonian-path gadgets would make the reduction,
+not the Moore/polarity-graph mathematics, carry the benchmark; it would also leave the
+required distributional Track A claim unproved. Randomly renumbering one known graph
+does not create new instances under the mandated canonical key.
+
+## Gate outcome
+
+| Requirement | Result | Evidence |
+|---|---:|---|
+| G — certificate known by construction | Possible for the Singer/polarity family | Constructions 3.33 and 3.34 |
+| H — Track A structural hardness | **Fail** | The paper gives an explicit O(N) certificate producer in the scalable, generatable regime |
