index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..e482afd50b4939f7bf9e111e31bd712cfb909bd1
--- /dev/null
+++ b/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,159 @@
+# Radio graceful labelings from hidden antipodal cycles
+
+Build status: **all local gates pass; the required oracle hardening is blocked by
+OpenRouter HTTP 403 key exhaustion.** The module and measurements are reproducible,
+but the LLM-hardness claim must not be treated as established until the script-owned
+bare, hinted, and placebo runs contain three genuine attempts rather than errors.
+
+| Profile field | Value |
+|---|---|
+| Track | A — structural hardness |
+| Native domain | combinatorics |
+| Object regime | finite discrete |
+| Computational core | graph |
+| Certificate | integer tuple (a vertex ordering) |
+| Intuition | reduction recognition: consecutive radio labels form an antipodal Hamiltonian path |
+| Domain essentiality | native |
+| Reduction | none |
+
+## The family
+
+The source is Cao, Dawkins, Hutchins, and Luce,
+[“Radio gracefulness of Moore graphs and beyond”](https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00228).
+An instance gives a diameter-two graph `G` succinctly by listing exactly its nonedges.
+The solver returns a bijection from its vertices to labels `1,...,n` satisfying
+`|f(u)-f(v)| + d(u,v) >= 3`.
+
+The listed nonedges form the antipodal graph `A(G)`. The generator first makes a
+planar cubic `A(G)` around a Hamiltonian cycle, adds two noncrossing perfect matchings,
+and randomly renumbers the vertices while carrying the cycle through the renumbering.
+Theorem 2.4 says a diameter-two graph is radio graceful exactly when its antipodal
+graph has a Hamiltonian path. Consequently, checking a submitted ordering requires
+only a permutation check and `n-1` exact edge-membership checks. No planted answer is
+read by `verify`.
+
+## Why Track A
+
+Theorem 2.4 makes the search exactly Hamiltonian-path search in a cubic planar
+antipodal graph. The relevant variable-size background regime is planar cubic
+Hamiltonicity, proved NP-complete by Garey, Johnson, and Tarjan. This worst-case result
+does **not** prove the planted distribution hard; the evidence for this distribution
+is the measured panel below.
+
+The easy paper regimes were excluded deliberately. Theorems 2.5 and 2.6,
+Proposition 3.4, and Porism 3.22 certify Hamiltonicity from high degree. Theorem 3.1
+makes even-diameter bipartite negatives elementary. Most importantly, Constructions
+3.33–3.34 generate Singer/polarity-graph labels by a direct linear recurrence; at the
+largest cap-compliant case that mechanical route and its compact route are the same,
+so it is not a Track B problem. Here the graph is known by construction but its hidden
+path is not exposed by vertex names or degrees.
+
+## Worked demo (`n=8`, seed `0`)
+
+The complete rendered instance is:
+
+```text
+Radio graceful labeling of a diameter-two graph
+
+Let G be the simple undirected graph with vertex set 0,1,...,7. Every pair of
+distinct vertices is an edge of G except for these unordered nonedges:
+
+2-3 0-1 3-7 1-7 4-5 1-3 5-6 0-4 0-5 4-6 2-7 2-6
+
+For vertices u and v, d(u,v) is the length of a shortest G-path. Find a
+bijection f to 1,...,8 satisfying |f(u)-f(v)|+d(u,v) >= 3 for every pair.
+Output [x1,...,x8], where xi is the zero-based vertex assigned label i, as:
+<answer>[...]</answer>
+```
+
+One answer is `[1, 0, 4, 5, 6, 2, 3, 7]`.
+`verify(inst, answer)` returns `(True, "ok")`; dropping its last entry returns
+`(False, "wrong_length:7_expected_8")`. This demo is hand-solvable: follow a path
+through the twelve listed nonedges. Exact enumeration finds 112 oriented valid
+labelings among `8! = 40,320` bijections.
+
+## Presets
+
+| Preset | `n` | Answer atoms | Status |
+|---|---:|---:|---|
+| demo | 8 | 8 | hand example; harden skips it |
+| easy | 120 | 120 | first oracle rung; calls blocked before attempts |
+| medium | 180 | 180 | local gates supported |
+| hard | 240 | 240 | provisional shipping preset; all local gates pass |
+
+## Gate results at `hard`
+
+| Gate | Result | Measurement |
+|---|---|---|
+| G1 | pass | planted answer and JSON round-trip, 16/16 preset-seed cases |
+| G2 | pass | drop, swap, duplicate, empty, and out-of-range rejected with 5 distinct reasons |
+| G3 | pass | tagged, fenced prose response round-trips and verifies |
+| G4 | pass | 0 hits / 200,000 uniform bijections; `P_hat=0` |
+| G5 | pass | strongest attack: 1,600,008 nodes, 185.914 s, 0/8; demo exact count 112 |
+| G6 | pass | all six attacks below failed on all eight seeds |
+| G7 | pass | doubled `n=480` instance built and verified |
+| G8 | pass | 60/60 relabel/order invariance checks, 60/60 transported witnesses, 20/20 unrelated keys distinct |
+| G9(c) | pass | 1,090 chars, about 273 tokens, 240 atoms, 240 intended traversal operations |
+
+The adversary panel contains degree/label outliers, least-onward greedy walks, 256
+randomized restarts, spectral seriation (4,502,400 counted operations), exact
+Hamiltonian-path DFS (800,008 nodes), and the construction-aware perfect-matching
+complement search (1,600,008 nodes). Every entry records `successes=0, attempts=8`.
+
+## Oracle loop and G9 arms
+
+The harness correctly refused to count provider errors as failures. The main bare
+ladder made four redraw calls at `easy`; all returned HTTP 403 before a genuine
+attempt. There is therefore no `hardened`/`too_easy` verdict and no oracle-selected
+shipping rung.
+
+| Run | Preset | Genuine solved/attempts | Script calls | Result |
+|---|---|---:|---:|---|
+| bare ladder | easy (`n=120`) | 0/0 | 4 | all HTTP 403 key-limit errors |
+| structural hint | hard (`n=240`) | 0/0 | 4 | all HTTP 403 key-limit errors |
+| placebo hint | hard (`n=240`) | 0/0 | 4 | all HTTP 403 key-limit errors |
+
+`hinted - placebo` is undefined because neither arm has a genuine attempt. The hint's
+effect on the claimed “reduction recognition” intuition is therefore unknown. The
+error-only script transcripts are retained as evidence of the blocker; they are not
+evidence that any model failed the problem.
+
+## Use
+
+```python
+from gen_2510_00228 import DIFFICULTY, make_instance, render, verify
+
+inst = make_instance(seed=7, **DIFFICULTY["hard"])
+print(render(inst))
+ok, reason = verify(inst, inst["answer"])
+assert (ok, reason) == (True, "ok")
+```
+
+From the repository root, after a successful oracle rerun establishes a shipping
+verdict, emit records with:
+
+```bash
+scripts/emit.sh 2510.00228 20 hard
+```
+
+## Caveats
+
+- `0/200,000` estimates density under the exact structure-aware prior of uniformly
+  random bijections. It is not an upper confidence bound of zero and says nothing
+  about a solver using graph structure.
+- Worst-case NP-completeness does not imply average-case hardness for this planted
+  two-page distribution. The attack panel is empirical and bounded.
+- The matching search and DFS stop at declared node budgets. No state-of-the-art SAT,
+  ILP/TSP solver, exhaustive unbounded search, or specialized planar-embedding
+  reconstruction was run. Any of these might expose an efficient distributional
+  attack.
+- The sparse antipodal graph is shown directly because it is the paper's central
+  Theorem 2.4 object and avoids printing the dense complement. This makes the
+  Hamiltonian-path reduction easier to recognize, not hidden from the solver.
+- The canonical key is a strong polynomial-time invariant, not a complete planar
+  graph isomorphism canonical form. It passed all required transformations and
+  distinctness tests, but a rare collision between nonisomorphic instances is
+  possible.
+- Most importantly, OpenRouter key exhaustion prevented STEP 4. This result is ready
+  for reproducible local audit but is **not ready to submit or call hardened** until
+  all three oracle arms are rerun successfully.
